# a-find-fetched-late — phase 1's LATE column, and the one that
# INVERTS the prediction.
#
# `a-find-nobody-fetched.keys` with a trip of its own bolted on at tick
# 141 — out to the wreck, back to the core, deliver — where
# `a-find-fetched-on-the-way.keys` takes the same wreck in passing at
# tick 8 for nothing.
#
#   nobody fetched     248 ticks
#   on the way         322 ticks       +74
#   this file          364 ticks       +116
#
# ⚠⚠ **`@X024` PREDICTED THE OPPOSITE** — *a find is a build accelerant
# and its value collapses once you are busy* — and on this base the late
# fetch is worth **half as much again** as the early one.
#
# ⚠⚠ **AND IT BUYS THAT WITH HALF THE LIFE**, which is the mechanism
# rather than the reading.  Both bodies are killed in the gate they are
# sent to:
#
#     delivered   stands up   killed        ALIVE     worth
#     tick 21     tick 112    187-202       ~80 tk    +74
#     tick 181    tick 272    302-317       ~40 tk    +116
#
# ⚠⚠ ***So what a body is worth is the PRESSURE it stands against and
# not the time it stands*** — about 0.9 ticks of base per tick alive
# early, and about 3 late, because the list ramps and the last waves are
# three times the first.  A find that is a BODY is spent by being used,
# so spending it early spends it on the cheap half of the base.
#
# ⚠⚠ **So the decay `@X024` describes is real but it is not a CLOCK, it
# is a MORTALITY** — what decays is not the opportunity to use the find,
# it is the find itself, and a ramping wave list makes the last hour of
# a body worth more than its first.  ⚠ `@M092` is where that is written
# down against `@X024` rather than over it: the design's claim is about
# a find that ACCELERATES A BUILD, and dryopea cannot measure one of
# those yet — see § The recon window below.
#
# ── ⚠⚠ AND THE LATE SORTIE OFTEN DOES NOT COME HOME ─────────────
#
# The fetch tick was swept at +0, +20, +40, +60, +80, +100, +120 and
# +140 past the opening trip.  **Four of the eight FAILED** — at +60,
# +80, +100 and +140 the player is killed carrying the wreck, and
# `vehicle_respawn` clears the carry (`plans/26` L2: *what reappears at
# the core is a repaired vehicle rather than the wreck that was
# destroyed*), so the find goes back on the ground and the trip bought
# nothing at all.  Tick 141 is a GAP between waves 2 and 3, and it is
# one of the four that works.
#
# ⚠ **That is the real late-fetch price, and it is a RISK rather than a
# discount**: the expected value falls because the trip stops being
# survivable, not because the reward shrinks.  ⚠ It is also the
# strongest form of `CLAUDE.md`'s design test — *does this put something
# in the player's hands at a moment when using it costs them
# something?* — because at wave three the cost is the vehicle.
#
# ── ⚠⚠ § The recon window, and why the BUILD half is unmeasurable ──
#
# `@X024`'s mechanism is *more hands for the whole session*, and the
# thing hands do early is BUILD.  dryopea cannot price that today:
# `@X022`'s recon window is FREE AND UNLIMITED — the list does not start
# until the player pokes — so a second builder before wave 1 saves the
# player's wall-clock and not one game-tick.  ⚠⚠ **The pressure `@X024`
# needs is the PERMIT's** (`@X025`, `ROADMAP.md` item 8), which is
# `EXPLORATION.md` § X2d's whole subject.  So phase 1's third finding is
# an ORDERING one: the incentive to scout EARLY does not exist until
# something is running while the player builds.

echo a find fetched late: a trip of its own at wave three, and worth MORE

do toggle_mode

do cycle_kind
click 0 0
marker 0 0 target

# Two towers, both EAST of the core, so the base is not mirror-symmetric
# and nobody is tempted to read it as one — `plans/14` H2 measured a
# 99-tick artefact on a map that looked mirrored, and `q -> -q` is not a
# symmetry of an odd-r lattice.
do cycle_kind
click 1 0
click 1 2
marker 1 0 tower
marker 1 2 tower

# ── ⚠ TWO spawn markers, and that is the load-bearing choice ────
#
# `plans/14` H2 measured a second crew member standing beside the first
# at worth **NOTHING** — 214 ticks against 214 — and the same person on
# the other front at +28.  ⚠⚠ **A crew is COVERAGE and not
# throughput**, so a ONE-front base would read the same in all three
# columns for a reason that has nothing to do with WHEN the find was
# collected.  That is `CLAUDE.md` § Testing something that moves' *a
# gate whose reading is already saturated cannot see what you built*,
# and it was caught here BEFORE the pair was written: a one-front
# version of this base read the same wallet to the decimal with the
# find, without it, and with a second crew member from tick zero.
do cycle_kind
do cycle_kind
do rotate
do rotate
do rotate
click 18 0
marker 18 0 spawn 3
do rotate
do rotate
do rotate
click -18 0
marker -18 0 spawn 0

count markers 5 5
do toggle_mode

# ⚠ The ground reaches (14, 0) and past it, because an unpainted hex IS
# sea and the find has to be stood on.
palette 5
drag -22 -2 22 -2
drag -22 -1 22 -1
drag -22 0 22 0
drag -22 1 22 1
drag -22 2 22 2
count painted 225 225

# A wall on each front with a GATE on row 0.  ⚠ The gate is not
# decoration: `plans/12` B7 measured a sealed wall at nearly double the
# clock and one with a gap in it at NOTHING, so a body standing in the
# gap is the whole of the front — and it is also the only way the
# player gets out, because a sealed base locks the player in as surely
# as it locks the crew out (`plans/13` V2).
palette 9
click 6 -2
click 6 -1
click 6 1
click 6 2
click -6 -2
click -6 -1
click -6 1
click -6 2
kind 6 0 grass
kind 6 1 wall
kind -6 0 grass
kind -6 1 wall

# The crew this base starts with, holding the EAST gate — the one the
# waves reach first.  The WEST gate has nobody, and that is the hole the
# find is fetched to fill.
crew 6 0
send 0 6 0
helper 0 6 0

# ── ⚠⚠ THE FIND — a crew member who is already down ─────────────
#
# `DESIGN.md` § 9's stranded helper, AUTHORED rather than played: a
# roster slot that is not standing, and their wreck lying on the hex
# they fell on, four hexes short of the spawn marker the player has to
# poke.  ⚠ Every mechanic this needs shipped in `plans/15` —
# `cargo_spawn`, `wave_take`, `wave_drop`, `helper_begin_recovery` —
# which is `EXPLORATION.md` § X7's whole point: **the first exploration
# scenario in dryopea is a `.keys` file, not a feature.**
crew 14 0
send 1 14 0
member 1 off 0.0 0.0 0.0
object 14 0 wreck 1 ground
roster 1 1
cargo 1 1

park 0 0
wallet 200.0 200.0

# ── The list — RAMPING, and that is deliberate ──────────────────
#
# ⚠⚠ A flat list saturates: `@M020`'s siege front is the wall's WIDTH,
# so past a certain size every column reads the same whatever is behind
# the wall (`@M085`, `@M087`).  A ramp gives the base an early half it
# survives and a late half it does not, which is what lets a body
# arriving at DIFFERENT moments be worth different amounts.
schedule 12 12 12 16 20 24 30 36
waves 0 0
count alive 0 0

# ── The opening trip, IDENTICAL in all three files ──────────────
#
# Out along the road, past the find, on to the marker, and home.  ⚠ The
# find is a leg of its own rather than a hex driven through, because a
# vehicle covers TWO hexes a tick and SKIPS every odd one — driving
# straight to (18, 0) never stands on the wreck and never stands on the
# marker either.
drive 14 0
tick 8
vehicle 14 0
drive 18 0
tick 3
vehicle 18 0
waves 1 1

drive 0 0
tick 10
vehicle 0 0

# ── Three waves in, and a gap to drive through ──────────────────
#
# ⚠ Wave 1 goes on the poke at tick 10, wave 2 at about 81 and wave 3 at
# about 181.  This trip leaves at 141, in the gap between the second and
# the third — which is the only reason it comes home.
tick 120

drive 14 0
tick 20
vehicle 14 0
take player

# ⚠ TWO on the ground now, because the crew member who was holding the
# east gate has been wrecked in it — this base loses one while the
# player is away fetching another.
cargo 2 2

drive 0 0
tick 20
vehicle 0 0
drop player

# ⚠ One left: the gate-holder's wreck, which nobody fetches in any of
# the three files.  `cargo` counts what is still IN THE WORLD, so the
# fall from two to one is the only line that says the delivery happened.
cargo 1 1

tick 91
roster 1 1
send 1 -6 0

fall 900
ticks 360 368
